WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.240
Rain and snow fall on a river basin.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:07.100
Part of it runs off the land.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:08.810
Part soaks into the soil.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:10.540
Part returns to the air.

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.830
And part takes the slow way to
the river, through the ground.

00:00:14.830 --> 00:00:17.732
A watershed model keeps
account of all this water,

00:00:17.732 --> 00:00:18.520
day by day.

00:00:20.370 --> 00:00:22.090
SWAT+ is such a model.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:27.428
It is the restructured version of SWAT,
the Soil and Water Assessment Tool,

00:00:27.428 --> 00:00:33.183
and it is developed by the USDA Agricultural
Research Service and Texas A&M AgriLife

00:00:33.183 --> 00:00:33.950
Research.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.427
People use it to ask what
a change does to a basin:

00:00:37.427 --> 00:00:40.898
another crop, another way of
tillage, a new reservoir,

00:00:40.898 --> 00:00:42.140
a warmer climate.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:47.275
The answers include streamflow, sediment
and nutrients for every day of many years,

00:00:47.275 --> 00:00:49.090
and the yield of every crop.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.190
SWAT+ does not treat
the basin as one piece.

00:00:53.390 --> 00:00:55.930
It divides it into objects
and connects them.

00:00:56.930 --> 00:00:58.280
The land comes first.

00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:02.060
Inside each subbasin, a
hydrologic response unit,

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.640
or HRU, is a share of the
area with one land use,

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.470
one soil and one slope.

00:01:07.670 --> 00:01:10.740
This is where the water balance
of the land is computed.

00:01:10.940 --> 00:01:14.800
Routing units collect the water
of their HRUs and pass it on.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:16.730
Channels carry it downstream.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:20.540
Aquifers lie below, and feed
the channels between storms.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:23.210
Reservoirs and ponds hold water back.

00:01:23.410 --> 00:01:28.030
Point sources add water from a file,
such as what a town or a plant releases.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:31.250
What the channels carry leaves
the basin at the outlet.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:35.910
The model of one basin can hold
thousands of these objects.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.810
Now follow the water through one HRU.

00:01:44.410 --> 00:01:45.660
The soil is a store.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:50.134
Each day, SWAT+ adds the
precipitation, as rain or as snowmelt,

00:01:50.134 --> 00:01:53.526
and takes away surface
runoff, evapotranspiration,

00:01:53.526 --> 00:01:56.050
and the water that seeps out below.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.090
The aquifer returns part of
that water to the channel.

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:02.489
That is the return flow,
the last term on screen;

00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:07.340
in SWAT+ the aquifer computes it, and
lateral flow also leaves the soil.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:09.580
How much runs off?

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:14.133
The curve number method answers from the
water that reaches the soil that day and

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.110
from one number, the curve number.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:20.920
Land use and soil set it, and it changes
each day with the water in the soil.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.370
The air takes water back.

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:26.923
SWAT+ first computes the
potential evapotranspiration,

00:02:26.923 --> 00:02:31.207
here from temperature and the radiation
at the top of the atmosphere,

00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:35.020
and then what the canopy, the
plants and the soil can give.

00:02:36.020 --> 00:02:38.610
Soil water above field capacity moves on.

00:02:38.810 --> 00:02:43.774
Some of it percolates down, layer by layer,
at a pace set by the conductivity of the

00:02:43.774 --> 00:02:44.220
soil.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:47.487
On a slope, some of it moves
sideways, as lateral flow,

00:02:47.487 --> 00:02:50.370
and goes through the routing
unit to the channel.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.515
What leaves the bottom of the
soil recharges the aquifer,

00:02:54.515 --> 00:02:58.764
which releases part of it slowly to the
channel while its water table is high

00:02:58.764 --> 00:02:59.300
enough.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.240
This flow keeps a river
running through dry weeks.

00:03:04.890 --> 00:03:08.330
In the channel, the water of
all the objects upstream meets.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:14.076
SWAT+ routes it from reach to reach: a
storage coefficient sets how much of the

00:03:14.076 --> 00:03:17.110
water in a reach leaves
it within the time step.

00:03:18.110 --> 00:03:19.740
Runoff also carries soil.

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:23.344
The sediment yield of an HRU
follows from the runoff,

00:03:23.344 --> 00:03:27.540
its peak rate, and factors for
soil, cover, practice and slope.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:31.010
This is the Modified
Universal Soil Loss Equation.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:37.051
Nitrogen and phosphorus travel the same
paths, dissolved in the water or bound to

00:03:37.051 --> 00:03:38.010
the sediment.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.910
What does a model need?

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.284
Weather for every day:
precipitation and temperature,

00:03:46.284 --> 00:03:50.380
and solar radiation, humidity
and wind, measured or generated.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:52.070
Soils, layer by layer.

00:03:52.270 --> 00:03:55.393
Land use, and how the land
is managed: planting,

00:03:55.393 --> 00:03:57.430
fertilizer, tillage, harvest.

00:03:57.630 --> 00:03:59.560
And the objects with their connections.

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:04.220
All of it is plain text in one model
folder, and one file lists the others.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:09.252
What comes out are tables: for
the whole basin, for every HRU,

00:04:09.252 --> 00:04:13.320
for channels, aquifers and
reservoirs, by day, month or year.

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:17.290
They hold the water balance,
with the water in the soil.

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:20.870
They hold the growth of the plants
and the yield of every crop.

00:04:23.470 --> 00:04:26.550
They hold the flow in every
channel, and the sediment,

00:04:26.550 --> 00:04:29.210
nitrogen and phosphorus
that leave the land.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:32.750
And they hold what aquifers and
reservoirs store and release.

00:04:33.750 --> 00:04:37.600
These plots show a few of their
columns; each table holds many more.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.379
SWATrust is a port of SWAT+
to the Rust language,

00:04:42.379 --> 00:04:45.040
with a launcher that runs in your browser.

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.860
The chapters of this
tutorial show how to use it.

